# Fire TV setup

This branch sideloads Streambert onto a Fire TV Stick and drives it with the remote. It is a personal install on the living-room Stick. It is not an Amazon Appstore build, and it does not target LG webOS.

The Stick does not ship the UI. The APK is a shell with two WebViews. The app WebView loads the Vite dev server on the Mac. Playback opens in a second fullscreen WebView, because the embed hosts refuse to be iframed.

## What has to be running

Put your own addresses in `firetv/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml` before you build. Do not commit them.

- Fire TV Stick: `YOUR_STICK_IP`. ADB is on.
- Mac on the same LAN: `YOUR_LAN_IP`
- App URL: `http://YOUR_LAN_IP:5173/?tv=1`

On the Mac:

```sh
npx vite --host
```

The Mac has to stay awake. If Vite is down, the Stick shows "Can't reach Streambert" and Select retries.

The TMDB token is the long Read Access Token, stored as `VITE_TMDB_API_KEY` in `.env`. That file is gitignored. `.env.example` only names the variable. Restart Vite after changing it.

If the Mac's LAN address changes, edit `start_url` in `firetv/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml` and rebuild the APK. The shell keeps using whatever URL was compiled in.

## Day to day

```sh
adb connect YOUR_STICK_IP:5555
adb -s YOUR_STICK_IP:5555 install -r firetv/streambert-tv.apk
adb -s YOUR_STICK_IP:5555 shell am force-stop com.streambert.tv
adb -s YOUR_STICK_IP:5555 shell am start -n com.streambert.tv/.MainActivity
```

Package name is `com.streambert.tv`. The activity is `MainActivity`. The signed APK is `firetv/streambert-tv.apk`. `firetv/.gitignore` ignores `*.apk`, `build/`, and `local.properties`, so the APK and the debug keystore stay on this machine.

Remote key codes, if you need to drive it from the Mac:

- 23 Select
- 19 Up
- 20 Down
- 21 Left
- 22 Right
- 4 Back

Chrome DevTools for the WebView:

```sh
PID=$(adb -s YOUR_STICK_IP:5555 shell pidof com.streambert.tv)
adb -s YOUR_STICK_IP:5555 forward tcp:9222 localabstract:webview_devtools_remote_$PID
```

Then open `http://127.0.0.1:9222/json`. The app page is the one on port 5173. While something is playing, a second page is the player WebView.

## How the shell works

`firetv/app/src/main/java/com/streambert/tv/MainActivity.java`

- Cleartext HTTP is allowed. The dev server is `http://` on the LAN.
- Leanback launcher category, so it shows up as a TV app. Touchscreen is not required.
- The Fire TV WebView scrolls on the D-pad instead of delivering arrow keys. The activity consumes the D-pad and injects `KeyboardEvent`s into the page.
- Back is Escape (key code 4). It closes the on-screen keyboard first, then a dialog, then the player, then one level up in the app.
- `window.StreambertTv.openPlayer(url)` shows the player WebView and hides the app WebView (`GONE`) so the show page cannot paint over the video. JavaScript on the app page stays resumed.
- Main-frame navigations off the embed host are blocked, so an ad tab cannot replace the movie. SSL errors on the player are cancelled.
- Aether, Vidnest, and Frembed get the same chrome-hiding CSS the desktop player uses (`src/ipc/siteTweaks.js`).
- Left and Rewind go back 10 seconds. Right and Fast-forward go forward 10 seconds. Select presses Skip intro when that button is on screen, and otherwise pauses. Play also pauses.
- The player WebView accepts Vidrift's window.open() check with a hidden popup. Without that, the first click (Skip intro, or a seek) replaces the movie with "Remove sandboxing to enable playback."

Desktop Electron still plays in `<webview>`. The shell path is only used when `window.StreambertTv.openPlayer` exists (`src/utils/tvShellPlayer.js`).

## Building the APK

`firetv/` is a normal Android project (compile SDK 34, min SDK 21, Java 17), and `./gradlew assembleDebug` is there. The APK on the Stick was built by hand. A full Gradle run filled the disk, so Java-only changes were compiled with `javac` and `d8` and zipped into an already-linked APK.

Tools on this Mac:

- Java 17: `/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/libexec/openjdk.jdk/Contents/Home`
- Android SDK: `~/Library/Android/sdk` (`firetv/local.properties`, gitignored)
- Build-tools 34.0.0, platform `android-34`

A resource or manifest change needs a fresh `aapt2` link. A Java-only change can reuse `firetv/build/manual/linked.apk`:

```sh
JAVA_HOME=/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/libexec/openjdk.jdk/Contents/Home
export PATH="$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH"
SDK=$HOME/Library/Android/sdk
BT=$SDK/build-tools/34.0.0
PLATFORM=$SDK/platforms/android-34/android.jar
ROOT=$PWD/firetv
OUT=$ROOT/build/manual

javac --release 17 -classpath "$PLATFORM" -d "$OUT/obj" \
  "$OUT/gen/com/streambert/tv/R.java" \
  "$ROOT/app/src/main/java/com/streambert/tv/MainActivity.java"

"$BT/d8" --min-api 21 --lib "$PLATFORM" --output "$OUT/dex" \
  "$OUT/obj/com/streambert/tv/"*.class

cp "$OUT/linked.apk" "$OUT/unsigned.apk"
(cd "$OUT/dex" && zip -q -j "$OUT/unsigned.apk" classes.dex)
"$BT/zipalign" -f 4 "$OUT/unsigned.apk" "$OUT/aligned.apk"
"$BT/apksigner" sign \
  --ks "$OUT/debug.keystore" --ks-pass pass:android --key-pass pass:android \
  --ks-key-alias androiddebugkey \
  --out "$ROOT/streambert-tv.apk" "$OUT/aligned.apk"
```

The debug keystore lives in `firetv/build/manual/` (gitignored). Password `android`, alias `androiddebugkey`. `Math.floorMod` is not used; min SDK 21 does not have it.

Do not commit `.env`.

## TV mode

`src/utils/tvMode.js`, booted from `src/main.jsx`.

TV mode turns on from `?tv=1`, from a Fire TV user agent, or from `localStorage` key `streambert_tv`. `?tv=0` turns it off.

The Stick's WebView lays the page out at 960×540 CSS pixels (device pixel ratio 2) and stretches that to the 1920×1080 panel, so every control was about twice as big as it should be. `width=1920` on the viewport was ignored. `initial-scale` works. The scale is `1.15 / devicePixelRatio` (0.575 on this Stick): the UI sits a bit above real panel pixels so type is readable, and the 10-foot text sizes in `src/styles/global.css` are bumped on top of that. The player is the other WebView, so video stays fullscreen.

`devicePixelRatio` is locked from the first reading. Changing the viewport can change the reported ratio, and an unlocked reading would keep rescaling.

Search and My Library stay at the larger pre-scale size. `--tv-restore` is `devicePixelRatio / 1.15` (about 1.739 here) and is applied as CSS `zoom` on `.search-box` and `.library-page`. The library page width stays `100%` so the zoomed page still fills the column beside the sidebar.

On a desktop browser, `?tv=1` uses the TV layout and a mild zoom. The Fire viewport scale only runs when `data-fire="1"`.

Focus is geometric (`src/utils/gamepadSpatialNav.js`), drawn with `.gamepad-focused`. The shell injects keys, so the old Silk "mouse moved, so navigate" path is off whenever `window.StreambertTv` exists. A raw Silk tab without the shell can still use that path.

## Remote behavior that was added

On-screen keyboard (`src/components/TvKeyboard.jsx`), not the Fire TV IME. It opens when Select or a click lands on a text field. Focusing the field is not enough. Back closes the keyboard before it leaves the page. Letters are written through the input's value setter and a bubbling `input` event so React sees the change.

If a search result is on screen, Up from the top letter row closes the keyboard and highlights the first match. Down still moves between letter rows, so A goes to G. After the keyboard closes, Up and Down walk the list and Select opens it. Left and Right move between keys. While the keyboard is open, the action keys show the matching Stick button. Rewind deletes a character, Fast-forward types a space, Play switches letters and numbers, and Menu toggles caps. Back still closes the keyboard.

Settings stays in its own column. Up and Down move through the options and do not jump to the sidebar. Left steps out to the sidebar on purpose. Back leaves Settings.

Playback source is in Settings, under Playback. The bar that said "Up to change source" on top of the video is gone. The choice is `streambert_playerSource`. The code default for non-anime is Vidrift. Aether was opening a black screen on this Stick because it had no file; Vidrift is the source that has been playing. Leave the video, pick a source, then start the episode again. Anime-only sources still fall back the way the show page already did.

My Library groups history by series. A series row shows how many episodes are in the history and the latest one. Select opens the episodes in season order. Each episode can be opened, marked watched, or removed. "Mark series watched" covers the episodes in that history, and "Remove series" clears the show. After a series is removed, focus moves to the previous row in the list. If that was the last thing in the library, the empty message takes focus instead of the sidebar.

Episode rows on a show are sized for a 1080p couch: a wide still, a large title, and a readable description. The list stays vertical so Down still walks episodes.

Home still has Continue Watching when an item has progress between 2% and 98% and is not marked watched. That row is hidden when nothing qualifies. The Fire TV player does not report a position the way the desktop webview does, so a title you only just started may show up in history before it shows up in Continue Watching.
